   "id": "p2-29",
   "name": "The greenhouse effect"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A greenhouse is a glass house for plants: sunlight passes through the glass, warms the inside, and the glass keeps much of the heat in.\nThe atmosphere works in a similar way. Sunlight reaches the ground and warms it; the warm ground gives out infrared radiation; some gases in the air (greenhouse gases: carbon dioxide, methane, water vapour) absorb part of it and keep the Earth warm.\nWithout the greenhouse effect the Earth would be too cold for life. But when people add more greenhouse gases (burning fuel, cutting forests, bush fires, rotting rubbish that gives methane), more heat is trapped: this is the enhanced greenhouse effect, the cause of global warming.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Why is the natural greenhouse effect useful but too much of it harmful?",
  "steps": [
   "Useful: it keeps the Earth warm enough for life.",
   "Harmful: extra greenhouse gases trap more heat and the Earth gets too warm."
  ],
  "answer": "It keeps us warm; too much warms the Earth dangerously.",
  "checks": []
 },
